 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
 - **Single Octave Limit**: A single-octave implementation (e.g., `fbm3` returning `0.5 * noise3(p)`) lacks the multi-scale detail of true FBM [S1, S14, unofficial].
-- **Precision**: High octave counts with high lacunarity can lead to floating-point precision issues at large coordinates. Using modulo arithmetic or tiling techniques can mitigate this [22470-tileable-noise-generation.md].
-- **Boundary Conditions**: Standard FBM is not periodic. For tileable volumes, the base noise must be tileable (e.g., using Fourier-domain techniques or coordinate wrapping) [22470-tileable-noise-generation.md].
